WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.929 --> 00:00:05.020
Hi, my name is Bradley Gillow.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:10.880
I’m a young filmmaker from Australia and
I’m currently directing a film called TO

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.340
CATCH A BUNYIP.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.960
When I was a kid I’ll always remember seeing
a camera around because my Dad used to video

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.510
tape all of my baby videos.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:27.510
I don’t know, it was something that I always
thought was interesting you know the phrase

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.670
A Picture Paints A Thousand Words.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:35.060
I like to imagine that film does that a thousand
times more than photography.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:39.100
That kind of sparked an interest for film
for me.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:46.060
By the time I was six I was just watching
movies constantly and the films that I would

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.390
watch the most were adventure films.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:57.120
TO CATCH A BUNYIP is basically a film about
this young girl Emma who goes to the Billabong

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:04.059
every day because she believes that the Bunyip
lives there and she wants to capture it for

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:11.770
its magic (she believes that Bunyips have
the power to regenerate life).

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:20.810
She wants to get the magic because she wants
to help her sick Mom Jess who suffers from

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.670
Parkinson’s Disease.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:31.300
Her older sister Abbey clashes with Emma because
she doesn’t believe in Bunyips and that

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.910
what Emma is saying is nonsense.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:37.350
She wants to have Emma share more responsibility.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.659
What made you want to tell this story?

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:48.150
Bradley: This film that I’m making is a
personal story that I’ve wanted to convey

00:01:48.150 --> 00:02:00.399
for about three years now because my Mom had
Parkinson’s Disease and it had a significant

00:02:00.399 --> 00:02:03.840
impact to the way that I was growing up as
a kid.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:12.500
When I graduated from high school my Mom passed
away from her 12-year battle with Parkinson’s

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.809
Disease.

00:02:13.809 --> 00:02:21.639
And for about two years I was depressed because
I wasn’t really sure where my life was going

00:02:21.639 --> 00:02:22.639
after that.

00:02:22.639 --> 00:02:33.530
I wanted to make this story to convey my feelings
on that experience as well as sort of an outlet

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:41.090
for me to get over those feelings and move
on.

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:50.510
The reason why I incorporated Bunyips, there
was a film that I watched when I was a kid

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:56.999
called Dot and the Kangaroo, that was a film
that I used to watch with my Mom.

00:02:56.999 --> 00:03:06.500
And I wanted to turn this bad situation into
something more light-hearted so I immediately

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.980
thought of that film.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.519
That film happens to feature a Bunyip.

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:25.129
I wanted to enjoy my inspiration from that
memory in particular and make a film where

00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:32.319
it comes from a very bad place into a more
light-hearted adventure film.

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:39.829
The story is not so much about the Bunyip
but rather the family.

00:03:39.829 --> 00:03:45.900
Yet the Bunyip is all a part of this and is
the link to the family.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:54.879
You don’t see the Bunyip per se, but I’ve
written it in a way that it’s left for interpretation

00:03:54.879 --> 00:04:02.430
whether the Bunyip exists or not and it’s
something for the audience to decide on.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:09.839
This is the first short film I’m directing.

00:04:09.839 --> 00:04:16.079
I’ve done directing in the past where I
was not 100% sure what I want or what I’m

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:24.270
going for but with this I have this clear
idea in terms of every small detail from production

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.270
design, cast, locations.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:33.430
I feel pretty confident as a director to do
my job well.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:43.669
I’ve spent so much time just thinking to
myself all the small details of how the scenes

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:51.229
are going to play out, how the shots are going
to look, how the performances are going to

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:55.840
play out during the scene.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.580
Obviously there are going to be issues that
are going to pop up while filming but I think

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:10.650
I have enough preparation to be ready for
the shoot and because of that I think that

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:16.360
this will turn out really good.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.210
In terms of the production of this film, so
far it’s been pretty challenging to say

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:26.500
the least because I wanted to have this film
set in the 1970’s.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:33.639
The probably with that is there’s not a
lot of locations that are still in that era.

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:40.389
So I was very worked up about finding the
right location that worked.

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:48.349
I feel like I’m finding places that are
perfect it’s just that it’s taking the

00:05:48.349 --> 00:05:54.479
time to find the right place that fits within
the film&nbsp; that would also have the right

00:05:54.479 --> 00:06:02.780
paints, textures and I think it’s worth
the wait of finding the right location rather

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:10.080
than shooting over at a mate’s place because
it shows that there’s a lot of attention

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.699
to detail.

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:20.129
During the course of our production it felt
like I was reliving those moments when my

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:29.330
Mom passed away because the way that I wrote
it was such a vivid part of that experience

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:39.470
and it was during those memories where I felt
alone during the course of our writing, developing

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:48.759
the story, finding locations, casting it felt
like I was very much in the moment of that

00:06:48.759 --> 00:07:00.270
pivotal day and when I pitched the film that
same night my Nan passed (who is my Mom’s

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:09.340
Mom) and it felt like I was reliving pretty
much those days.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:17.889
But it’s good in a way because I believe
with that I think I’ll be able to direct

00:07:17.889 --> 00:07:25.270
my head-of-departments and my actors to bring
them with me in those days.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:33.449
I’m definitely sorting things through and
I have a really strong team behind me who

00:07:33.449 --> 00:07:35.800
makes this film run smoothly as well.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:43.150
It definitely helps me knowing that they’re
there for me as well.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:49.069
As I said before this story is about family
rather than the adventure that the protagonist

00:07:49.069 --> 00:07:50.169
is having.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:58.090
So for me it was important to find people
that looked relatively the same.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:01.389
The youngest girl Eva is brilliant.

00:08:01.389 --> 00:08:08.349
Eva was the second girl we auditioned for
the protagonist role of Emma.

00:08:08.349 --> 00:08:16.330
I immediately told the producers that she
was the one and we pretty much casted her

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:17.330
straight after that.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:26.550
One of the characters in my film (Jess) I
wrote her to intentionally have Parkinson’s

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:34.050
disease and this character is very important
to me because she is based on my own Mom and

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:41.550
I wanted to get my feelings off my chest into
this one character that means a lot for this

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:43.910
story and for myself.

00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:52.699
I wanted to find someone who was really good
at body performances and someone who was willing

00:08:52.699 --> 00:08:58.740
to study how Parkinson’s affects you mentally
as well as psychically.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:05.940
And we came across Mel and she was very genuine
and it felt like she was perfect.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:11.940
The rest of the cast was great, as well to
help sell the idea that everyone is a family

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:19.930
and I believe they’re perfect.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:27.180
This film is sort of independently raised
at the moment out of my Mother’s inheritance

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:37.680
because I believe that might as well use what
she gave me to help make her film.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:44.690
The end goal with this film (like I said before)
I kind of want this film to be viewed as as

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:52.470
a quintessential film for Parkinson’s disease
where the patients can watch and it’s something

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:54.850
that they can relate to.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:10:02.350
I think the most important thing about this
film starts with the story because that’s

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:08.350
pretty much the blueprint of any film and
if you don’t have a good blueprint than

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:09.470
you don’t have a good film.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:19.120
I made sure to make the script as tight and
as vivid as possible so when someone reads

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:26.399
it they get a good sense on how everything
is going to play out and I made sure that

00:10:26.399 --> 00:10:34.579
I’ll leave room for actor’s interpretations
so that they can get into their roles more

00:10:34.579 --> 00:10:40.410
and it doesn’t have to be dialogue fro the
script but something that comes from themselves

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.259
personally.

00:10:43.259 --> 00:10:48.180
I feel like I’m not in the position to give
anyone advice because I’m still learning

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:53.250
but make films that you personally want to
make.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:59.839
I don’t think you have to personally experience
something to make a good film.

00:10:59.839 --> 00:11:07.330
If you’re willing to put in time and effort
to research into a particular idea I think

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:13.560
if you believe in that film then it could
be possible to make it happen and make it

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.019
a really good film.

00:11:15.019 --> 00:11:16.910
One more question.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.180
Bradley: Yes.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.180
Sure.

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:20.700
Are Bunyips real?

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:28.240
Bradley: I like to think so but I haven’t
seen one.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.529
I’m sure they’re out there…somewhere.

